Here are several ways to remove duplicates from an array in JavaScript:

## Method 1: Using Set (Most Common and Efficient)

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    return [...new Set(arr)];
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 4];
const uniqueNumbers = removeDuplicates(numbers);
console.log(uniqueNumbers); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

## Method 2: Using filter() and indexOf()

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    return arr.filter((item, index) => arr.indexOf(item) === index);
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 4];
const uniqueNumbers = removeDuplicates(numbers);
console.log(uniqueNumbers); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

## Method 3: Using reduce()

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    return arr.reduce((unique, item) => {
        return unique.includes(item) ? unique : [...unique, item];
    }, []);
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 4];
const uniqueNumbers = removeDuplicates(numbers);
console.log(uniqueNumbers); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

## Method 4: Using forEach() with a Map/Object

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    const seen = new Map();
    const result = [];

    arr.forEach(item => {
        if (!seen.has(item)) {
            seen.set(item, true);
            result.push(item);
        }
    });

    return result;
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 1, 4];
const uniqueNumbers = removeDuplicates(numbers);
console.log(uniqueNumbers); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

## Recommended Approach

**Method 1 using `Set`** is the most efficient and widely used approach because:
- It has O(n) time complexity
- It's concise and readable
- It works well with primitive values (numbers, strings, booleans)
- It preserves the order of first occurrences

The `Set` object automatically stores only unique values, and the spread operator (`...`) converts it back to an array.

